---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { meta } from '../data/meta';
import { icons } from '../lib/icons';
import { dataSources, furtherReading } from '../data/sources';
import { armorPieces } from '../data/armor';
import { decorations } from '../data/decorations';
import { charms } from '../data/charms';
import { skills } from '../data/skills';

const description =
  'HunterForge data sources and methodology: game-file-extracted Monster Hunter Wilds data via wilds.mhdb.io, sharpness multipliers cited from the community, snapshot dates and known gaps listed in the open.';

const kindCounts = {
  armor: skills.filter((s) => s.kind === 'armor').length,
  weapon: skills.filter((s) => s.kind === 'weapon').length,
  set: skills.filter((s) => s.kind === 'set').length,
  group: skills.filter((s) => s.kind === 'group').length,
};
---

<Base title="Data Sources & Methodology" description={description} path="/about">
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: 'Data & Sources' }]} />
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">Honesty page</p>
    <h1>Data Sources &amp; Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      If a number on this site looks wrong, this page tells you exactly where it came from, when it was
      taken, and what it deliberately leaves out.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="stat-strip">
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">{meta.snapshotDate}</div><div class="lbl">data snapshot</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">{armorPieces.length}</div><div class="lbl">HR armor pieces</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">{skills.length}</div><div class="lbl">skills</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">{decorations.length}</div><div class="lbl">decorations</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">{charms.length}</div><div class="lbl">charm ranks</div></div>
  </div>

  <section class="card" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
    <h2>Sources fetched for this snapshot</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="data">
        <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>What it provided</th><th class="num">Accessed</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {dataSources.map((s) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="display:inline-flex; align-items:center; gap:.3rem;">
                  {s.name} <Fragment set:html={icons.external} />
                </a>
              </td>
              <td style="color:var(--bone-dim);">{s.what}</td>
              <td class="num">{s.accessed}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.85rem; color:var(--bone-faint); margin:.8rem 0 0;">
      Further reading (not scraped for the dataset): 
      {furtherReading.map((s) => (
        <a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="display:inline-flex; align-items:center; gap:.3rem; margin-right:.6rem;">
          {s.name} <Fragment set:html={icons.external} />
        </a>
      ))}
 - the wiki.gg community wiki blocks automated access, so HunterForge links to it for cross-checking instead of crawling it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid-2">
    <section class="card">
      <h2>What's in the dataset</h2>
      <ul style="color:var(--bone-dim); padding-left:1.1rem; margin:0;">
        <li><strong>{armorPieces.length} high-rank armor pieces</strong> - every piece in the game files at snapshot time: skills, decoration slots (512 pieces have slots), defense, resistances and crafting materials.</li>
        <li><strong>{skills.length} skills</strong> - {kindCounts.armor} armor, {kindCounts.weapon} weapon, {kindCounts.set} set-bonus and {kindCounts.group} group skills, each with max level and per-level effects straight from the game text.</li>
        <li><strong>{decorations.length} decorations</strong> - {meta.coverage.armorDecorations} armor jewels and {meta.coverage.weaponDecorations} weapon jewels with slot requirements.</li>
        <li><strong>{charms.length} charm ranks</strong> in {meta.coverage.charmRanks > 0 ? '64' : '-'} families, with crafting materials.</li>
        <li><strong>{meta.coverage.armorSets} set bonuses</strong> and 17 group skills with activation thresholds.</li>
        <li><strong>14 weapon presets</strong> (one per weapon class) for the ERaw calculator.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section class="card">
      <h2>Known gaps - shown, not hidden</h2>
      <ul style="color:var(--bone-dim); padding-left:1.1rem; margin:0;">
        <li><strong>Low-rank armor is excluded.</strong> The tools target endgame high-rank building; LR pieces exist in the source but aren't in the builder.</li>
        <li><strong>Decoration crafting materials are missing upstream.</strong> wilds.mhdb.io does not expose them, so the planner links each jewel to the wiki instead of guessing materials.</li>
        <li><strong>Weapon decorations can't be placed.</strong> The builder manages armor slots only - weapon slots depend on the equipped weapon, which is out of scope.</li>
        <li><strong>Elemental sharpness multipliers are reference-only.</strong> Community sources disagree on the Blue value (1.05 vs 1.0625); it's marked disputed wherever shown.</li>
        <li><strong>Some charms are event/limited</strong> and marked not craftable in the source data.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>

  <div class="tape" role="presentation"></div>

  <div class="grid-2">
    <section class="card">
      <h2>Methodology</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--bone-dim); margin-bottom:.5rem;">
        Datasets are generated, not hand-typed: a script downloads the mhdb snapshots into
        <span class="mono">data-raw/</span>, validates them (every skill reference must resolve, slot
        levels must be 1–3, thresholds 2–4) and emits typed TypeScript modules. The validation runs again
        in the test suite alongside 41 unit tests covering skill aggregation, caps, set/group bonuses,
        deco fitting and the ERaw formula.
      </p>
      <p style="color:var(--bone-dim); margin:0;">
        Modeling decisions worth knowing: set bonuses pool pieces of the same family across α/β/γ variants
        (the common case; the few variant-specific bonuses like Gogmazios pool separately), and group
        skills pool by skill name across different sets - both matching in-game behavior.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="card">
      <h2>Update cadence &amp; corrections</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--bone-dim); margin-bottom:.5rem;">
        The snapshot is dated in the banner on every page and in the footer - it does not silently change.
        Title updates to Wilds add armor and jewels; when the pipeline re-runs against a fresh mhdb
        snapshot, the date and counts update together, and shared links built against removed items keep
        working by dropping the unknown pieces <em>and saying so</em> instead of failing.
      </p>
      <p style="color:var(--bone-dim); margin:0;">
        Monster Hunter Wilds is Capcom's; this is a fan tool with no affiliation. If a number disagrees
        with the game, check it against the wiki links on each tool page - that's what they're for.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <div class="tape" role="presentation"></div>

  <section class="card" aria-label="Who makes HunterForge">
    <h2>Who makes HunterForge</h2>
    <p style="color:var(--bone-dim); max-width:75ch;">
      HunterForge is an independent, single-maintainer fan project - no company, no ads, no sponsor.
      It exists because Wilds deserved build tools whose numbers you can trace: dated snapshots, cited
      sharpness values, and gaps labeled in the interface instead of papered over. Corrections and dataset
      discrepancy reports are welcome at <a href="mailto:admin@hunterforge.top">admin@hunterforge.top</a>;
      for anything gameplay-related, the community wiki links on every tool page are the authoritative
      cross-check. This page (and the <a href="/changelog">changelog</a>) is updated whenever the dataset
      or the math changes - dates are only bumped for substantive updates.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
